The path of a sample
--------------------
client (AudioQueue / AudioUnit / IOProc)
  -> AudioConverter in the client: its format to interleaved float at the device's rate
  -> the client's shared ring (sf_audio_ring, 4 periods of 512 frames, float)
  -> coreaudiod, one thread per device: sums one period of every running client, applies
     the software level when the device has no hardware volume, clips, rounds to S16LE
  -> write() to /dev/sfaudio -> the driver's FIFO -> DMA ring -> CS42L83

Nothing of this needs IOAudioFamily. An engine of the new kind is made for every node of
sDevTable that exists, by a poll thread (every 200 ms, so a node published after coreaudiod
started is found), and only while no IOAudioEngine is there; the IOAudioFamily code is as it
was (three places ask "is this a device engine": applyVolume, hostTimeOfFrame, startIOThread,
and the software gain now looks at the device's flags, which say the same for the family's
engines).

Rates: the device runs at 48000 Hz (DEV_RATE; coreaudiod sets kern.sf_sound.rate when the
driver is idle and keeps whatever the driver has if it cannot). A client at 44100 Hz is
converted in the client: the ring a client gets is at the device's rate, and AudioQueue and
the output units put an AudioConverter (linear interpolation) in front of it. coreaudiod's
own converter (mixClient, also linear) runs only for a ring whose rate differs from the
device it lands on, which happens when the default output moves to a device at another rate
under a client that follows the default. Both are linear interpolation without a low-pass:
good enough for system sounds, audibly rough on dense high-frequency material; a better
resampler belongs in AudioConverter.c and mixClient, not here.

Pacing and timestamps
---------------------
The drivers' FIFOs take 16384 frames (341 ms) before a write blocks. Filling them would make
every sound a third of a second late, so the server keeps DEV_QUEUE_FRAMES (3072, 64 ms)
queued instead: <prefix>.fifo_frames says how many written frames the driver has not taken
yet; the thread reads it (one sysctl by MIB) and writes a period of 512 whenever the queue
has room for one, else sleeps 2 ms. The device's clock is therefore the only clock: no
drift between host time and the codec, and AudioQueue callbacks run at the device's rate
because the rings empty at it (measured from a client: 48006 and 44112 frames/s).

  start: the headphones' driver begins a session with about 1.1 s of silence while the
    codec's converter locks and does not read its FIFO meanwhile: the queue stays full,
    the server waits, the clients are held and their first frames are not lost. The
    speakers start with the first frames. Nothing has to be inferred about either.
  underrun: an empty queue found while clients play (after the first frames of a run) is
    counted and logged ("ran out of frames"); nothing is made up for.
  stall: a queue that stays full for 3 s closes the node; it is tried again 5 s later.
  errors: a write the driver refuses (ENXIO once the kernel has shut the speakers'
    amplifiers down for good) closes the node for 5 s; a driver whose state sysctl says
    off, failed, disabled or codec-only is no device: the poll thread takes the device
    out of the table within 200 ms (clients on the default move to what is left) and puts
    it back when the state allows.
  idle: with no client playing nothing is written (the drivers play silence while the node
    is open); DEV_LINGER_SECONDS (5) after the last client the node is closed, so a new
    sound within that time starts at once and a later one pays the driver's start.
  timestamps: readHostTime of a client's ring is now + (queued + 512 + DEV_OUTPUT_FRAMES)
    frames, DEV_OUTPUT_FRAMES (5120) being the periods the drivers fill ahead of the DMA.
    The device's latency is reported as 8192 frames (171 ms).

A device without fifo_frames (the /dev/null stand-in, an older collection) is paced by the
host clock: one period per period time, write() blocking when the device's queue is full.

Volume
------
Headphones: kSFAudioDeviceHardwareVolume | HardwareMute. Level 0..1 maps linearly to the
codec's attenuation, kern.sf_sound.volume_db = round((1 - level) * 62): 0 dB at 1.0, 62 dB
at 0.0; mute writes 63 (the driver's mute) and keeps the level. At the first start the level
is read back from the driver (30 dB -> 0.516), so starting the server changes nothing that
can be heard; later starts use /Library/Preferences/org.siliconforest.audio.plist.
A device without hardware volume (speakers) is scaled in the mix, level squared, and starts
at 0.5 (DEV_SOFT_VOLUME: -12 dB) until a level is set.

The speakers
------------
    { "Speakers", "SFAudio:speakers", "/dev/sfspeaker", ..., "kern.sf_speaker", false, kDevJackEmpty }
A second engine of the same kind, with the software level. The server never touches the
amplifiers' volume and never opens /dev/sfspeaker-safety: whether the speakers can be heard
at all is the kernel's and the safety daemon's business (without the daemon: -100 dB), and
the server's level is not a protection.

The default output follows the jack (devPollThread/devRouteLocked): every 200 ms
kern.sf_sound.jack is read; when it changes, or a device comes or goes, the default output
and the default system output become the kDevJackPlugged device (Headphones) while
something is plugged (1 headphones, 2 headset) and the kDevJackEmpty device (Speakers)
otherwise, each only when that device exists. A default set by hand
(kSFAudioSetDefaultOutput) holds until the next jack event.

What happens to running clients at a switch: a client on the default device (every
AudioQueue and default output unit: the alias device 0x40) keeps its ring and its rate; at
its next period the other device's thread mixes it. The device left is closed 5 s later;
the device taken is opened at once (for the headphones: 1.1 s of start during which the
client is held, nothing is dropped; what was already written to the device left is played
there, up to 64 ms plus the driver's periods). A client that connected to a device by its
id (an IOProc on that device: sftone -u) stays on it. Listeners get
kAudioHardwarePropertyDefaultOutputDevice and the alias device's volume and mute change
notifications, as for any default change.

Test hooks, environment of coreaudiod: COREAUDIOD_JACK_FILE=<file> reads the jack's number
from a file instead of the sysctl (the jack is empty in every unattended boot);
COREAUDIOD_SPEAKER_DEVICE=<node> names a stand-in for /dev/sfspeaker (/dev/null works).

Adding capture
--------------
(Written at the pin. At the tip upstream's coreaudiod, CoreAudio and AudioToolbox have the input
side for IOAudioFamily engines: input rings, deliverInput(), kSFAudioDeviceHasInput, the connect
message's input word. What is left for the /dev backend is the driver node and a reading thread
that calls deliverInput(); the framework work listed below is what upstream added (not exercised here).)

Driver: a read-only node, say /dev/sfmic: S16LE at kern.sf_mic.rate, channels fixed and
told by a sysctl (kern.sf_mic.channels), read() blocking until frames are there, capture
running only while the node is open; kern.sf_mic.frames (captured) and .overruns. The
headset microphone (kern.sf_sound.jack == 2) would be a second node.

coreaudiod (small): a direction in dev_desc; an engine with kSFAudioDeviceHasInput and
inputChannels; a thread that reads periods, converts to float and writes them into the ring
of every client connected for input, signalling its semaphore. The ring is the one that
exists with the roles turned round (the server moves writeFrame, the client readFrame; a
client that does not read loses the oldest frames). The connect message has a spare word
(pad) for the direction; kSFAudioFollowDefaultInput next to kSFAudioFollowDefaultOutput.
Default input, kSFAudioSetDefaultInput and inputChannels are already in the protocol. The
node is opened while an input client runs, as for output.

Frameworks (the larger part; today there is no input anywhere):
  CoreAudio   AudioHardware.c answers input-scope properties with "no property"
              (streams, stream configuration, stream format, latency): they need the
              input side of the device info. AudioDeviceIO.c calls IOProcs with a NULL
              input list: it needs an input session per device and to hand each cycle's
              captured frames to the procs.
  AudioToolbox AudioQueueNewInput returns kAudioQueueErr_InvalidDevice. It needs the
              mirror of the output queue: an IOProc on the default input device, an
              AudioConverter from the device's float to the queue's format, filling the
              enqueued buffers and calling the input callback with the packet count.
              The output unit (AUHAL) needs kAudioOutputUnitProperty_EnableIO on element 1
              and an input callback with AudioUnitRender from the captured frames.

Known limits
------------
  - One writer: /dev/sfaudio opens once. While coreaudiod holds it sfplay gets EBUSY, and
    while something else holds it coreaudiod retries every 5 s.
  - An AudioQueue always plays on the default output (the tree's AudioQueue accepts
    kAudioQueueProperty_CurrentDevice and ignores it); a client that wants one device
    uses an IOProc on it.
  - kern.sf_sound.tone and a second user of the driver's rate are outside the server's
    knowledge: the driver's test tone is not heard while the server writes (the driver
    plays its FIFO first), and a rate changed while the node is closed is set back at the
    next open.
  - The output is clipped, not limited, when clients sum above full scale.

The desktop
-----------
What a desktop session must have for sound (all from `nix-build build-sysaudio.nix -A all`,
staged as tools/sa_boot.sh does):
    usr/sbin/coreaudiod                                         -> /usr/sbin/coreaudiod
    System/Library/Frameworks/CoreAudio.framework/Versions/A/CoreAudio      -> same path
    System/Library/Frameworks/AudioToolbox.framework/Versions/A/AudioToolbox -> same path
    System/Library/Frameworks/AudioUnit.framework/Versions/A/AudioUnit      -> same path
    usr/bin/sfvolume, usr/bin/sftone                            -> /usr/bin (optional tools)
and one process, started once before or after the compositor, as root, no arguments, no
environment (never COREAUDIOD_JACK_FILE or COREAUDIOD_SPEAKER_DEVICE outside a test):
    mkdir -p /var/log /Library/Preferences
    /usr/sbin/coreaudiod > /var/log/coreaudiod.log 2>&1 &
It needs the SFAppleSound collection (kern.sf_sound, /dev/sfaudio; with the speakers
kern.sf_speaker, /dev/sfspeaker) and finds the nodes whenever they appear. For the speakers
to be heard the safety daemon must run as well; that is not part of this.

Volume keys (sources/chonkstep-darwin, crates/wm-darwin/src/volume.rs): F10 mute, F11
quieter, F12 louder by a sixteenth, without modifiers, on the default output; the notice
says "Volume 56%" or "Sound off". The compositor sends coreaudiod's own Mach messages
(get state, set volume, set mute) itself. Starting /usr/bin/sfvolume instead was the
alternative: the compositor starts programs only through its managed-spawn shim, which is
an admission path for applications, and a process per key press costs a dyld start in the
input path; a lookup and two messages cost well under a millisecond, need nothing staged
and fail at once when coreaudiod is not there (the keys are then ordinary F-keys). The
price is that volume.rs repeats the layout of SFAudioServer.h's state and set messages.
